.TH TALLY 1 "July 2026" "tally 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tally, ty \- print newline, word, and byte counts for each file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tally
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.br
.B tally
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fB\-\-files0\-from\fR=\fIF\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tally
is a from-scratch reimplementation of GNU
.BR wc (1)
(parity target: coreutils 9.11) with SIMD counting kernels. Given the same
input and flags it produces byte-identical output \(em same counts, same
column widths, same diagnostics (modulo the program name), same exit codes
\(em while running substantially faster on every measured workload.
.B ty
is the same binary under a shorter name.
.PP
Print newline, word, and byte counts for each FILE, and a total line if
more than one FILE is specified. A word is a nonempty sequence of non white
space delimited by white space characters or by start or end of input.
.PP
With no FILE, or when FILE is \-, read standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
The options below select which counts are printed, always in the following
order: newline, word, character, byte, maximum line length.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-bytes
print the byte counts
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-chars
print the character counts
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lines
print the newline counts
.TP
.B \-\-debug
indicate which counting kernels are in use
.TP
.B \-\-files0\-from=\fIF\fR
read input from the files specified by NUL-terminated names in file F;
if F is \-, read names from standard input
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-max\-line\-length
print the maximum display width
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-words
print the word counts
.TP
.B \-\-total=\fIWHEN\fR
when to print a line with total counts; WHEN can be: auto, always, only,
never
.TP
.B \-\-help
display help and exit
.TP
.B \-\-version
output version information and exit
.SH EXTENSIONS
Flags outside GNU wc's surface live under the
.B \-\-tally\-*
namespace and never change default behavior.
.TP
.B \-\-tally\-threads=\fIN\fR
count large regular files with
.I N
parallel readers (\fBauto\fR selects one per online CPU, capped at 8).
Applies to line, word, and character counting; \fB\-L\fR, pipes, devices,
and small files fall back to the serial path. Output is byte-identical to
a serial run. The
.B TAL_THREADS
environment variable sets the same value; the flag wins.
.SH LOCALES
Word separators and character counts follow the current locale exactly as
GNU wc does: the libc's
.BR iswspace (3)
classification plus the non-breaking-space family (U+00A0, U+2007, U+202F,
U+2060) unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set. Invalid byte sequences count toward words but not characters.
Display widths for \fB\-L\fR come from
.BR wcwidth (3).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if any file could not be read (counts for readable files
are still printed).
.SH DEVIATIONS
tally matches GNU wc byte-for-byte with these documented exceptions:
the program-name token in diagnostics; \fB\-\-help\fR/\fB\-\-version\fR
text; \fB\-\-debug\fR informational output; "total" is not translated; and
word counts are independent of read fragmentation \(em GNU wc 9.11
miscounts words when adjacent multibyte spaces are split across read
boundaries (reported upstream), tally does not.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B tally \-l src/*.c
newline counts and a total for the sources
.TP
.B ty big.log
lines, words, and bytes \(em the default set
.TP
.B find . \-print0 | tally \-\-files0\-from=\-
count every file found, NUL-safe
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wc (1)
.PP
Source and measured benchmarks: https://github.com/tenseleyFlow/tally
